<%# PREVIEW - a single detail page holding both the school's attributes and its years.
    Not merged: `edit` still exists and still works. Compare /admin/schools/:id with /admin/schools/:id/edit.

    **What the field ships.** Stripe's customer page, Linear's project page, GitHub's repository settings and
    Polaris's resource detail pages all hold a record's attributes *and* its related collections on one
    screen, with editing in place. None of them has a separate edit screen for a record with one attribute -
    that is a Rails scaffold convention, and it earns its place for a long form.

    The saving is what changes, not just the layout: this page has **two independent writes**. The name form
    saves the name; the years manage themselves. So there is no single "Save" that means everything on screen,
    which is the thing to look at - Polaris solves it with a contextual save bar that appears when a field is
    dirty, and Stripe with per-section saves. This preview uses per-section, which is the smaller mechanism. %>
<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
  <%= admin_breadcrumbs(@breadcrumbs) %>

  <%= render layout: "components/ui/page_header",
             locals: { title: @school.name,
                       description: "Everything about this school, on one page." } do %>
    <%= link_to "Delete school", admin_school_path(@school),
                data: { turbo_method: :delete, turbo_confirm: delete_confirm(@school) },
                class: admin_danger_button_class %>
  <% end %>

  <div class="space-y-6">
    <%# **The years first.** Measured on the first draft of this preview, with Details above them, they
        started at y=618 of a 625px viewport - below the fold, on the page you came to them for. That is the
        same cost `classrooms#show` already paid: adding to the top of a page costs the thing the page is for.
        A one-field Details section is not worth the 280px it put above them. %>
    <%= render "school_years", school: @school, school_years: @school_years,
                               addable: @addable_years, manage: true %>

    <%# A visible heading, matching create. It was `sr-only` on the grounds that one field needs no heading,
        and that is true in isolation and wrong here: create shows the same section, and a heading on one page
        and not the other is the inconsistency this shape exists to remove.

        The hint carries the one genuinely confusing thing about a page that mixes a form with immediate
        actions: **what saves when.** Details waits for the button; the years above apply as you click. %>
    <section aria-labelledby="details-heading">
      <h2 id="details-heading" class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900">Details</h2>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600">Saved when you press Save details.</p>

      <div class="mt-3">
        <%= render "form" %>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# **No "Record" card.** It held an id and two timestamps in a 160px card: the id is already in the URL,
        and a school's `created_at` is not something anybody acts on. design.md: a card earns its edges for a
        summary figure, a person or a preview. Where the field shows this at all it is a muted metadata line -
        and Stripe shows an id because you need it for the API, which has no equivalent here. %>
  </div>
</div>
